--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Replacing an asset
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every widget takes the asset it uses as a prop — <LaptopReveal> takes
`modelUrl` — so nothing here is baked in. If you point a widget at your own
file and stop shipping ours, that asset's obligations stop applying to your
build, and you can delete its entry below the line in your fork.

Do not delete an entry while still shipping the file it describes. For CC-BY
in particular, dropping the credit is the one thing the licence does not
allow, and a bundler will not warn you.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Adding an asset
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before committing a new model, texture, font or sound:

  1. Check the licence actually permits redistribution inside an npm package,
     and commercial use. "Free to download" is not a licence. CC-BY, CC0 and
     the permissive code licences are fine; CC-BY-NC and most marketplace
     "standard" licences are not.

  2. Check it for third-party marks. A model of a real product usually
     carries the manufacturer's logo in its textures, and a trademark is not
     covered by the asset's own licence however permissive that licence is.
     Remove the mark, or do not ship the asset.

  3. Add an entry above in the same shape: file path, which widget uses it,
     licence with URL, the credit verbatim as the author worded it, and every
     change made to the original. Record measurements the runtime depends on
     in asset.extras, as laptop.glb does.

  4. If the licence is share-alike (CC-BY-SA, GPL-family), stop and think
     first — it can reach further than the asset and is usually the wrong
     choice for a package other people embed.


